[
  "<blockquote class=\"masthead__epigraph\" lang=\"en\">\n          <p class=\"masthead__epigraph-text\"><svg class=\"masthead__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Life beyond utility is the domain of sovereignty.</p>\n          <cite class=\"masthead__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Georges Bataille</b>, The Accursed Share, vol. III</cite>\n        </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Nature is an endless combination and repetition of a very few laws.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Ralph Waldo Emerson</b>, Essays: First Series — History (1841)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>An infinite sphere whose centre is everywhere and whose circumference is nowhere.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Blaise Pascal</b>, Thoughts, article I (1670)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>So, naturalists observe, a flea\nHas smaller fleas that on him prey;\nAnd these have smaller still to bite &#39;em,\nAnd so proceed ad infinitum.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Jonathan Swift</b>, On Poetry: a Rhapsody (1733)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Changed, I rise again the same.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Jakob Bernoulli</b>, Epitaph, Basel Minster (1705)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>The love that moves the sun and the other stars.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Dante Alighieri</b>, The Divine Comedy, Paradise XXXIII (1321)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Nothing new under the sun.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Clementine Vulgate</b>, Ecclesiastes 1:10 (1592)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Windmills, not giants.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Miguel de Cervantes</b>, Don Quixote I, chapter VIII (1605)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>If every tool could do its own work, at a command or by anticipating what was needed…</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Aristotle</b>, Politics I 1253b</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Divide each difficulty into as many parts as possible and as necessary to resolve it.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>René Descartes</b>, Discourse on Method, part II (1637)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>One yin, one yang: this is called the Way.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Commentaries on the Book of Changes</b>, Appended Statements I.5</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>The Gion bells echo the passing of all things; the sal trees’ blossoms tell how the mighty must fall.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>The Tale of the Heike</b>, Book I, Gion Monastery</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>And thou canst not stir a flower\nWithout troubling of a star.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Francis Thompson</b>, The Mistress of Vision (1897)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Build thee more stately mansions, O my soul,\nAs the swift seasons roll!\nLeave thy low-vaulted past!</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Oliver Wendell Holmes</b>, The Autocrat of the Breakfast-Table, chapter IV (1858)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Everything changes; nothing perishes.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Ovid</b>, Metamorphoses XV.165 (8)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>A harmony that turns back upon itself.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Heraclitus</b>, Fragment B51, in Hippolytus, Refutation IX.9.2</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Order is the arrangement that assigns to things equal and unequal their proper places.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Augustine of Hippo</b>, The City of God XIX.13 (426)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>From six triangles, one equilateral triangle is formed.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Plato</b>, Timaeus 54e</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>The senses do not deceive; judgement deceives.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Johann Wolfgang von Goethe</b>, Maxims and Reflections (1908)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>No eye could ever see the sun without becoming sunlike.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Plotinus</b>, Enneads I.6.9</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>They would take nothing for truth but the shadows of manufactured things.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Plato</b>, Republic VII 515c</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Every branch of a plant, every limb of an animal, every drop of its fluids is itself such a garden or pond.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</b>, Monadology, 67 (1714)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Another reaction system in two dimensions gives rise to patterns reminiscent of dappling.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>A. M. Turing</b>, The Chemical Basis of Morphogenesis (1952)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>The river flows unceasingly, yet its water is never the same. Foam on the pools vanishes and forms, never staying long.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Kamo no Chōmei</b>, An Account of My Hut (1212)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Scents, colours and sounds answer one another.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Charles Baudelaire</b>, The Flowers of Evil, Correspondences (1861)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>If atoms never swerved, all would fall like drops of rain through the deep void…</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Lucretius</b>, On the Nature of Things II.221–222</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>One must still have chaos within to give birth to a dancing star.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Friedrich Nietzsche</b>, Thus Spoke Zarathustra, Prologue 5 (1883)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>A one-sided surface is formed, bounded by a single line…</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>August Ferdinand Möbius</b>, On Polyhedra and Elementary Transformations (1858)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>There are more prime numbers than any given multitude of primes.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Euclid</b>, Elements IX.20</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>A point can move continuously so that, in a finite time, it reaches every point of a region of the plane.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>David Hilbert</b>, Mathematische Annalen 38 (1891)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>The eyes cannot know the nature of things.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Lucretius</b>, On the Nature of Things IV.385</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Why do they not fall with five sides, or seven? Why always six?</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Johannes Kepler</b>, The Six-Cornered Snowflake (1611)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>This makes the theorem more beautiful and more concise…</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>René Descartes</b>, Letter to Elisabeth, November 1643 (1643)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Every point of a luminous body—the sun, a candle, a glowing coal—generates waves of which it is the centre.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Christiaan Huygens</b>, Treatise on Light (1690)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Small differences in the initial conditions may produce very great differences in the final phenomena…</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Henri Poincaré</b>, Science and Method (1908)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>The same circle surrounds the dodecahedron’s pentagon and the icosahedron’s triangle when both solids are inscribed in one sphere.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Hypsicles</b>, Elements XIV.2</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Thirty spokes share one hub; it is the emptiness that makes the cart useful.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Laozi</b>, Tao Te Ching, 11</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>The unreal has no being; the real never ceases to be.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Bhagavad Gita</b>, The Yoga of Knowledge, 2.16</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Turn it and turn it again, for everything is in it.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Ben Bag Bag</b>, Ethics of the Fathers 5:22</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>As a flexible chain hangs, so, inverted, a rigid arch will stand.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Robert Hooke</b>, The Life of Dr. Robert Hooke, p. xxi (1705)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>The lines MS, NO and PQ will be of the same order: this will be a third lemma.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Blaise Pascal</b>, Essay on Conics, third lemma (1640)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>The stone the builders rejected has become the cornerstone.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Psalms</b>, Psalm 118:22</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Suddenly Julien stopped speaking of Napoleon; he announced his plan to become a priest…</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Stendhal</b>, The Red and the Black, chapter V (1831)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>A mighty Maze! but not without a Plan;</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Alexander Pope</b>, An Essay on Man, Epistle I, line 6 (1736)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Given any three points, lines or circles, draw a circle through each given point, if any, and tangent to each given line or circle.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Pappus of Alexandria</b>, Collection VII, §11</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>A square and ten of its roots equal thirty-nine dirhams.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi</b>, The Compendious Book on Calculation</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>That point moves back and forth along the diameter of the larger circle through the initial point of contact.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Nasir al-Din al-Tusi</b>, Memoir on Astronomy II.11</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>The same tower appears round from afar and square from nearby.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Sextus Empiricus</b>, Outlines of Pyrrhonism I.118</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>They will always be seen together on the circumference of circles growing successively larger.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Galileo Galilei</b>, Two New Sciences, third day (1638)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>On the surface of a cylindroid, though doubly curved, infinitely many straight lines can be drawn.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Christopher Wren</b>, Philosophical Transactions, no. 48 (1669), p. 962 (1669)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Taking each in turn, he went as far as the root of seventeen; there, somehow, he stopped.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Plato</b>, Theaetetus 147d</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Like perspectives, which rightly gazed upon show nothing but confusion; eyed awry, distinguish form.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>William Shakespeare</b>, Richard II, II.2 (1597)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>The voyage of the best ship is a zigzag line of a hundred tacks. … See the line from a sufficient distance, and it straightens itself to the average tendency.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Ralph Waldo Emerson</b>, Essays: First Series — Self-Reliance (1841)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Why is it that in eclipses of the sun, if one looks through a sieve or through leaves, such as a plane tree’s … the beams on the ground become crescents?</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Pseudo-Aristotle</b>, Problems XV.11</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>If, as the saying goes, man is a bubble, all the more so is an old man.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Varro</b>, On Agriculture I.1.1</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Life, like a dome of many-coloured glass,\nStains the white radiance of Eternity,\nUntil Death tramples it to fragments.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Percy Bysshe Shelley</b>, Adonais, stanza LII (1821)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>Heard melodies are sweet, but those unheard\nAre sweeter;</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>John Keats</b>, Ode on a Grecian Urn, stanza II (1820)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>If more than two regions have an odd number of bridges leading to them, such a passage cannot be made.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Leonhard Euler</b>, Geometry of Position, §20 (1741)</cite>\n              </blockquote>",
  "<blockquote class=\"card__quote\" lang=\"en\">\n                <p class=\"card__quote-text\"><svg class=\"card__quote-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><use href=\"#icon-quote-left\"/></svg>The Moving Finger writes; and, having writ,\nMoves on: nor all thy Piety nor Wit\nShall lure it back to cancel half a Line,\nNor all thy Tears wash out a Word of it.</p>\n                <cite class=\"card__cite\">—&nbsp;<b>Omar Khayyam</b>, Rubaiyat</cite>\n              </blockquote>"
]
